DATABASE C:/Users/melwa/GitHub/ENTICES/Core11_idealgas_mod_v4.dat
TITLE Test B2 -- corrected Hdg carbon-decoupling test
#
# WHY THIS SUPERSEDES test_B_Hdg_carbon.pqi
#   Running the original found two defects that made its headline comparison
#   (case 1 vs case 2) meaningless:
#
#   1. Cases 1 and 2 were INITIAL SOLUTION calculations only. PHREEQC honours
#      input valence-state totals in an initial solution and does NOT force
#      cross-element redox equilibrium there, so pe stayed at the input
#      default (4) and NO carbon reduced in the coupled case. The control
#      therefore failed to reproduce the artefact it existed to reproduce.
#      A batch-reaction step is required to get "pe ... Adjusted to redox
#      equilibrium". (The original's cases 3/4 accidentally did this.)
#
#   2. Cases 3 and 4 declared Magnetite/Pyrrhotite/Fe(OH)2 at "0 0" (SI 0,
#      ZERO moles) in a solution containing NO Fe and NO S. PHREEQC reported
#      "Element not present" for all three and they did nothing. So the
#      "realistic version with a reducing mineral assemblage" never had a
#      mineral assemblage. The claim in OPTION3_decision.md sec 2 that with
#      Hdg "pe is then set by the mineral assemblage" was therefore NOT
#      tested by that file.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE TESTS
#   Sim 1  coupled H(0),  no minerals      -- the artefact control
#   Sim 2  decoupled Hdg, no minerals      -- does decoupling stop it?
#   Sim 3  coupled H(0),  REAL Fe assemblage (Fe and S in solution, real moles)
#   Sim 4  decoupled Hdg, REAL Fe assemblage -- what sets pe now?
#   Sim 5  decoupled Hdg + Ca + Calcite    -- does carbon still see carbonates?
#
#   Deciding number: log10(CH4/CO2), and pe, in 2 vs 1 and 4 vs 3.
#   Sim 4 is the one that matters most for the design: if pe there is
#   unconstrained or absurd, "let the minerals set pe" is not established.

SELECTED_OUTPUT 1
    -reset false
    -simulation true
    -pH true
    -pe true
    -totals C Hdg Fe S Ca
    -molalities CH4 CO2 HCO3- CO3-2 H2
    -si Calcite Magnetite Pyrrhotite

# ===========================================================================
# SIM 1 -- COUPLED H2, no minerals, WITH a batch-reaction step so redox
#   actually equilibrates. Halite is a deliberate no-op trigger: strongly
#   undersaturated at these concentrations and present at 0 moles, so it
#   transfers nothing, but its presence forces the batch-reaction calculation
#   that closes the electron balance.
# ===========================================================================
SOLUTION 1 coupled-H2 reducing
    temp      1.45
    pH        11.5
    units     mol/kgw
    Na        0.1
    Cl        0.1 charge
    C         1e-3
    H(0)      1e-3
    water     1
EQUILIBRIUM_PHASES 1
    Halite    0 0
END

# ===========================================================================
# SIM 2 -- DECOUPLED Hdg, otherwise identical to sim 1.
# ===========================================================================
SOLUTION 2 decoupled-Hdg
    temp      1.45
    pH        11.5
    units     mol/kgw
    Na        0.1
    Cl        0.1 charge
    C         1e-3
    Hdg       1e-3
    water     1
EQUILIBRIUM_PHASES 2
    Halite    0 0
END

# ===========================================================================
# SIM 3 -- COUPLED H2 with a REAL reducing Fe(II) assemblage.
#   Fe and S are present in solution AND the phases have real mole
#   inventories, so they can buffer (dissolve), not only precipitate.
# ===========================================================================
SOLUTION 3 coupled-H2 with minerals
    temp      1.45
    pH        11.5
    units     mol/kgw
    Na        0.1
    Cl        0.1 charge
    C         1e-3
    Fe        1e-5
    S(-2)     1e-4
    H(0)      1e-3
    water     1
EQUILIBRIUM_PHASES 3
    Magnetite   0 1e-2
    Pyrrhotite  0 1e-2
END

# ===========================================================================
# SIM 4 -- DECOUPLED Hdg with the SAME real assemblage.
#   THE KEY CASE: with H2 removed from the electron balance, is pe now set
#   sensibly by magnetite/pyrrhotite, or is it unconstrained?
# ===========================================================================
SOLUTION 4 decoupled-Hdg with minerals
    temp      1.45
    pH        11.5
    units     mol/kgw
    Na        0.1
    Cl        0.1 charge
    C         1e-3
    Fe        1e-5
    S(-2)     1e-4
    Hdg       1e-3
    water     1
EQUILIBRIUM_PHASES 4
    Magnetite   0 1e-2
    Pyrrhotite  0 1e-2
END

# ===========================================================================
# SIM 5 -- Carbonate check: with Hdg in play, does carbon still participate
#   in calcite saturation? (Confirms the design's advantage over decoupling
#   carbon itself, which would lose carbonate minerals entirely.)
# ===========================================================================
SOLUTION 5 decoupled-Hdg with Ca
    temp      1.45
    pH        11.5
    units     mol/kgw
    Na        0.1
    Cl        0.1 charge
    Ca        1e-3
    C         1e-3
    Hdg       1e-3
    water     1
EQUILIBRIUM_PHASES 5
    Calcite     0 0
END
